{
  "concept": "In the Hall-Héroult cell, Al2O3 dissolved in molten cryolite is electrolyzed so that Al3+ is reduced to molten aluminum at the carbon cathode floor while O2- is oxidized at the consumable carbon anodes, producing O2, CO, and CO2 gas.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Outer steel shell (dark grey) drawn as an open-topped rectangular vessel in cross-section",
    "Ceramic insulating liner (salmon/terracotta) inside the steel shell",
    "Green electrolyte body labeled 'Al2O3 dissolved in molten Na3AlF6'",
    "Pale green solid 'Crust' band across the top surface of the electrolyte",
    "Two large black rectangular carbon anodes labeled 'Carbon anode (+)' suspended into the melt",
    "Grey metal anode support yokes/stems rising from each anode to a horizontal busbar",
    "Horizontal positive busbar at top right terminating in a '+' terminal",
    "Black sloped 'Carbon cathode (−)' slab forming the cell floor, thicker on the left, tapering to the right",
    "Negative lead bar exiting to the left terminating in a '−' terminal",
    "Silvery-grey 'Molten aluminum' layer pooled between the electrolyte and the cathode floor, tap spout at right",
    "Clusters of white circular bubbles hugging the sides and bottoms of both anodes, labeled 'Bubbles of O2, CO, and CO2'",
    "Vertical exhaust duct lines rising from the cell hood with an upward arrow",
    "Annotation 'HF and particulates exhaust to filter plant'",
    "Leader lines and labels: 'Ceramic', 'Steel shell' on the left; 'Crust', 'Bubbles of O2, CO, and CO2', 'Al2O3 dissolved in molten Na3AlF6', 'Molten aluminum' on the right",
    "Migrating ion markers (shown when animating): red Al3+ spheres drifting downward to the cathode, blue O2- spheres drifting toward the anodes",
    "Half-reaction caption panel: anode 'C(s) + 2 O2−(l) → CO2(g) + 4 e−' and cathode 'Al3+(l) + 3 e− → Al(l)'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "electrolysisAnimation",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Run electrolysis",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "Charge carriers move in opposite directions: Al3+ migrates down to the negative carbon cathode and is reduced to liquid metal, while O2- migrates to the positive carbon anodes, is oxidized, and leaves as rising gas bubbles."
    },
    {
      "id": "appliedCurrent",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Applied current (kA)",
      "range": [0, 300, 10],
      "default": 150,
      "teaches": "Current sets the rate of electron transfer, so more current means faster bubble generation at the anodes and a faster-thickening molten aluminum pad — Faraday's law made visible."
    },
    {
      "id": "electrodeFocus",
      "type": "click",
      "label": "Inspect a cell part",
      "teaches": "Each labeled region has a distinct chemical role: the anode is consumed, the cathode collects product, the cryolite lowers the melting point, and the crust and exhaust handle containment and HF emissions."
    },
    {
      "id": "anodeWear",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Anode condition",
      "options": ["Fresh anodes", "Consumed anodes"],
      "default": "Fresh anodes",
      "teaches": "The carbon anodes are reactants, not inert hardware — they burn away as CO/CO2 and must be periodically replaced."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The cell at rest",
      "narration": "With no current applied, the cell is just a lined steel pot holding green molten cryolite with Al2O3 dissolved in it, two carbon anodes dipped in from above, and a carbon cathode floor. A solid crust caps the melt.",
      "state": { "appliedCurrent": 0, "electrodeFocus": null, "anodeWear": "Fresh anodes" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why cryolite?",
      "narration": "Click the green electrolyte: pure Al2O3 melts near 2000 °C, but dissolving it in molten Na3AlF6 lets the cell run near 960 °C, which is what makes the process industrially possible.",
      "state": { "appliedCurrent": 0, "anodeWear": "Fresh anodes" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Switch on the current",
      "narration": "Turn on the animation at moderate current. Red Al3+ ions drift down to the negative cathode floor and are reduced to molten aluminum; blue O2- ions travel to the positive anodes and are oxidized.",
      "state": { "appliedCurrent": 150, "anodeWear": "Fresh anodes" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Gas at the anodes",
      "narration": "Bubbles of O2, CO, and CO2 stream off the anode faces because the liberated oxygen attacks the carbon itself. HF and particulates rise through the hood to the filter plant.",
      "state": { "appliedCurrent": 150, "anodeWear": "Fresh anodes" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Crank up the current",
      "narration": "Push the current to 300 kA: bubbling becomes vigorous and the silvery aluminum pad on the cathode floor thickens noticeably faster — product mass scales with charge passed.",
      "state": { "appliedCurrent": 300, "anodeWear": "Fresh anodes" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The anodes are consumed",
      "narration": "Switch to 'Consumed anodes'. The carbon blocks have shrunk because they are oxidized along with the O2-; real smelters replace anodes on a regular schedule.",
      "state": { "appliedCurrent": 300, "anodeWear": "Consumed anodes" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The carbon anode is not a spectator — it is a reactant that is burned away to CO and CO2 as the oxide ion is oxidized, which is why aluminum smelting consumes carbon blocks and emits carbon gases while depositing pure molten metal on the cathode floor.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Cross-sectional 2D diagram, wider than tall. Outer open-topped vessel drawn as a thick dark-grey steel shell with a salmon/terracotta ceramic liner just inside it. The interior is filled with a medium olive-green electrolyte block topped by a slightly paler green horizontal crust band. Two identical tall black rectangles ('Carbon anode (+)', white bold text) hang side by side into the green, each supported by grey forked metal stems that rise to a long horizontal white/outlined busbar running right to a '+' sign. Dense clusters of small white circles hug the vertical sides and bottom edges of both anodes. Below the green layer sits a metallic silver gradient band ('Molten aluminum') that tapers and exits at the right as a tap spout. Under it, a black wedge sloping downward left-to-right spans the cell floor, labeled 'Carbon cathode (−)' in white bold; a horizontal outlined bar exits to the left ending in '−'. Two thin vertical duct lines rise from the top of the shell to a bold upward arrow, annotated 'HF and particulates exhaust to filter plant'. Leader lines with plain black text: left side 'Ceramic' and 'Steel shell'; right side 'Crust', 'Bubbles of O2, CO, and CO2' (two leaders converging on both anode bubble clusters), 'Al2O3 dissolved in molten Na3AlF6', 'Molten aluminum'. For interaction states: ion markers are small red spheres (Al3+) descending through the green toward the cathode and small blue spheres (O2-) converging on the anodes; bubble spawn rate and aluminum layer thickness both scale with the current slider; the consumed-anode toggle shortens and narrows the black anode rectangles and raises them slightly out of the melt. A small caption panel below or beside the cell shows the two half-reactions, color-matched red for the cathode reduction and blue for the anode oxidation."
}

